{"name":"Copper Rose","hex":"#996666","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose"},{"title":"Copper (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a reddish brown color that resembles the metal copper. The first recorded use of copper as a color name in English was in 1594."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Copper-18706.jpg/330px-Copper-18706.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_(color)"},{"title":"Rose (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Rose is the color halfway between red and magenta, whose hue corresponds to an angle of 330 degrees on the HSV/HSL color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg/330px-Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/1280px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A copper disc (99.95% pure) made by continuous casting; etched to reveal crystallites","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACu-Scheibe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/M